## A singular release of a Hot Wheels model

A Hot wheels car represents a particualar model as well as a particular release of said model i.e. a model can have multiple releases over the years with different colors, designs etc. For reference, this will be referencing the [Oscar Mayer Wienermobile](https://hotwheels.fandom.com/wiki/Oscar_Mayer_Wienermobile) model.

```json
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Thing",
  "name": "Oscar Mayer Wienermobile",
  "description": "Did you know that there is a real Wienermobile that travels the country and weighs exactly the same as 1 million hot dogs?",
  "url": "https://hotwheels.fandom.com/wiki/Oscar_Mayer_Wienermobile",
  "releaseDate": "1996",
  "manufacturer": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "Mattel",
    "url": "https://www.mattel.com/"
  },
  "material": [
    {
      "@type": "Text",
      "@value": "Zamac (zinc alloy)"
    },
    {
      "@type": "Text", 
      "@value": "ABS plastic"
    }
  ]
}
```

DTCG (Design Tokens Community Group) has defined a design token specification in JSON syntax. The [specification](https://www.designtokens.org/tr/2025.10/) is divided into three modules: [Format](https://www.designtokens.org/tr/2025.10/format), [Color](https://www.designtokens.org/tr/2025.10/color/), and [Resolver](https://www.designtokens.org/tr/2025.10/resolver/). `Format` provides the general details, `Color` provides the syntax specifically for color tokens, and `Resolver` provides how to define multiple contexts under which the tokens can be "resolved".

## Base colors

This example define three [base color tokens](https://www.designtokens.org/tr/2025.10/color/#base). DTCG tokens are infinitely nestable to create appropriate hierarchies. Here, we have a `color` token that contains a `palette` token that contains a `gray` token that contains three color definitions.

```json
{
  "color": {
    "palette": {
      "gray": {
        "1000": {
          "$type": "color",
          "$value": {
            "colorSpace": "oklch",
            "components": [1, 0, 0],
            "alpha": 1,
            "hex": "#ffffff"
          }
        },
        "900": {
          "$type": "color",
          "$value": {
            "colorSpace": "oklch",
            "components": [0.9911, 0, 0],
            "alpha": 1,
            "hex": "#fcfcfc"
          }
        },
        "0": {
          "$type": "color",
          "$value": {
            "colorSpace": "oklch",
            "components": [0, 0, 0],
            "alpha": 1,
            "hex": "#000000"
          }
        }
      } 
    }
  }
}
```

Their equivalent CSS variables would end up as:

```css
:root {
  --color-palette-gray-1000: oklch(1 0 0);
  --color-palette-gray-900: oklch(0.9911 0 0);
  --color-palette-gray-0: oklch(0 0 0);
}
```
